EXPERIMENT-4.1
AIM-Given the head of a singly linked list, return the middle node of the linked list.If there are two middle nodes, return the second middle node.

ALGORITHM:
1. Start
2. Initialize two pointers:
   slow = head
   fast = head
3. Traverse the linked list using the condition:
   fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr
4. In each iteration:
   Move slow one node forward: slow = slow->next
   Move fast two nodes forward: fast = fast->next->next
5. When fast reaches the end of the list, slow will be pointing to the middle node.
6. Return slow.
7. Stop

CODE:
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* middleNode(ListNode* head) {
        
        ListNode* slow = head;
        ListNode* fast = head;

        while (fast != nullptr && fast->next != nullptr) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        } 

        return slow;
    }
};

LERANING OUTCOMES:
1. Understand and implement linked list traversal.
2. Learn the slow and fast pointer technique.
3. Find the middle element of a linked list efficiently.
4. Understand how two pointers can traverse a data structure at different speeds.
5. Analyze the time complexity: O(n) and space complexity: O(1).
6. Improve problem-solving skills for linked-list-based problems.

EXPERIMENT-4.2
AIM:Given the head of a singly linked list, return true if it is a palindrome or false otherwise.

ALGORITHM:
1. Start
2. Create an empty stack st.
3. Set a temporary pointer:
   temp = head
4. Traverse the linked list and push each node's value into the stack.
5. Reset temp = head.
6. Traverse the linked list again:
   Compare temp->val with st.top().
   If they are different, return false.
   Otherwise, remove the top element using st.pop().
   Move temp to the next node.
7. If all values match, return true.
8. Stop

CODE:
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
        stack<int> st;
        ListNode* temp = head;
        while (temp != nullptr) {
            st.push(temp->val);
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        temp = head;
        while (temp != nullptr) {
            if (temp->val != st.top()) {
                return false;
            }
            st.pop();
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        return true;
    }
};


LEARNING OUTCOMES:
1. Understand the concept of a palindrome in a linked list.
2. Learn to traverse a linked list using pointers.
3. Learn to use a stack for storing and comparing elements.
4. Understand the LIFO (Last In, First Out) property of a stack.
5. Develop problem-solving skills using linked lists and stacks.
6. Understand the time complexity of the algorithm as O(n).
7. Understand the space complexity of the algorithm as O(n).
